@node WhatsNew "What's new in 1.4.0"
@toc MAIN
                           What's new in 1.4.0
                           -------------------

@{b}MintVID 1.4.0@{ub}

  - @{b}Stereo audio.@{ub} Paula now plays through a synchronized
    left/right channel pair, so stereo sources keep their stereo image
    instead of being mixed down to one channel. Mono sources, and the Mono
    audio option, play the same signal on both speakers.
  - @{b}PiStorm 600 plays YouTube.@{ub} On the tested Pi3-based PiStorm
    600 (MintVID040), YouTube's 360p stream plays with VQ Turbo and the
    new RTG (Half) display mode. The YUV-to-RGB step fell from 57 ms to
    about 12 ms per 640x360 frame there, and H.264 decode is faster too.
  - @{b}RTG (Half)@{ub} display mode: the window opens at half the video's
    width and height and colour conversion writes straight to that size,
    so conversion and the copy to the card each do about a quarter of the
    work (H.264).
  - @{b}VQ: Smoosh@{ub} keeps H.264 moving on a machine that cannot keep
    up: late P/B pictures are not decoded, motion smears until the next
    keyframe, and audio always comes first.
  - @{b}Video: Off@{ub} plays only the soundtrack; no video is decoded.
    See @{" Video frame policy " LINK FramePolicy}.
  - @{b}Skip after@{ub} sets how far behind Video: Skip Frames may fall
    before it starts skipping.
  - @{b}Turbo+ keeps its audio.@{ub} It no longer skips about 10 s of
    audio while waiting for the next keyframe.
  - @{b}Faster decoding.@{ub} H.264 motion compensation and Baseline
    (YouTube) decode need fewer instructions - up to about a fifth less
    on high-motion video - and AAC is decoded directly at a halved or
    quartered output rate (31-63% less AAC work).
  - @{b}P96 hardware overlay on real Voodoo3/Permedia boards.@{ub} It opens
    with the packed-YUV format the historical RiVA driver used, with the
    right chroma order (the new @{i}P96 Output Format@{ui} menu switches
    between YVYU for WinUAE and YUYV for Voodoo) and studio-range
    clamping. The overlay window can be resized with the card doing the
    scaling, and fullscreen falls back to CGX if a board refuses it.
  - @{b}Bigger buffers.@{ub} Fast buffer adds 32 and 64 MB, and YouTube/
    IPTV HLS downloads several segments ahead of playback.

Performance still depends on the exact CPU, source, codec, resolution,
audio and display mode. A supported format is not automatically a
real-time format.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Overview "Overview"
@toc MAIN
                                 Overview
                                 --------

@{b}What MintVID is@{ub}

MintVID plays video files, YouTube, and IPTV channels directly on the
Amiga. It is built in the spirit of MintAMP (the libhelix-based audio
player): a small portable C core with thin Amiga-specific display and
audio layers around it. MP3/AAC decoding reuses the proven MintAMP/Helix
code, with audio output through Paula.

The goal is to go beyond MPEG-1 on real accelerated Amiga hardware.
MintVID provides a broad range of codecs, but codec support does not imply
real-time playback on every supported CPU. Practical performance depends
on processor speed, codec complexity, resolution, bitrate and display
mode:

  - 68030-class ECS/AGA systems - best suited to lightweight codecs and
    modest frame sizes. Cinepak is the natural starting point; heavier
    formats may decode correctly without being practical in real time.
  - 68040/060 systems - older codecs such as Cinepak, MJPEG, MPEG-1/2,
    MPEG-4 Part 2 and WMV7/8 become more practical at modest resolutions,
    especially with RTG. H.264/AVC remains extremely demanding, but 1.2.0
    brought the lowest-resolution BBC One HLS stream close to real time on
    a tested real 68060 using AGA/HAM8. Higher resolutions remain beyond
    normal classic hardware, and results depend on the exact stream and
    configuration.
  - PiStorm/Emu68 (ARM-hosted 68k) - use the MintVID040 build. H.264/AVC
    (Baseline through High Profile, CABAC and B-frames) becomes much more
    practical. On the tested Pi3-based PiStorm 600, YouTube's 360p stream
    plays with VQ Turbo and the RTG (Half) display mode. Faster PiStorm
    hardware should provide more headroom, but exact results depend on the
    stream and configuration.
  - Vampire/Apollo 68080 - not yet validated by the MintVID project. No
    optimised build is officially recommended yet, and the MintVID060
    build should not be assumed to be correct solely from the CPU name.

MintVID uses the same feature set across its 68030, 68040 and 68060
builds. For normal classic systems use MintVID030, MintVID040 or
MintVID060 for the matching CPU. PiStorm/Emu68 uses MintVID040. There is
no separate AGA, RTG or PiStorm feature edition; display mode and
practical playback performance depend on the hardware underneath it.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Installation "Installation"
@toc MAIN
                               Installation
                               ------------

@{b}Installing MintVID@{ub}

For a normal 68030, 68040 or 68060 system use the MintVID030, MintVID040
or MintVID060 drawer respectively. PiStorm/Emu68 users should use
MintVID040. Vampire/Apollo 68080 has not yet been validated, so there is
no official optimised-build recommendation for it yet. Copy the chosen
drawer to your Amiga (or a shared PiStorm volume) and keep its contents
together.

Run @{b}MintVID@{ub} on systems with ReAction V44. A standard AmigaOS 3.0
system without ReAction V44 should instead run @{b}MintVID-GT@{ub}, the
plain GadTools/Intuition equivalent; it opens the matching -GT browsers
automatically. IPTV and YouTube browsing are reachable from the
controller's menus.

Everything else in the drawer (mrplay, iptvgui, ytgui, the GadTools -GT
set, mr_decode) is a support binary MintVID or the -GT controller loads on
demand. Nothing in the release drawer is intended to be split up or copied
out on its own.

@{b}Requirements@{ub}

  - AmigaOS 3.0 or later.
  - 68030 or better.
  - AmiSSL v5 + amisslmaster.library for HTTPS, YouTube and online IPTV
    features when the build was compiled with SSL=1.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Controls "Controls"
@toc MAIN
                                 Controls
                                 --------

@{b}Playing a video@{ub}

Run the controller, choose a movie, and select a display mode: AGA, ECS,
EHB, HAM6, HAM8, RTG (WritePixel), RTG (Half), P96 (Windowed) or P96
(Fullscreen). Laced and 2x apply to the chipset modes; RTG playback opens a
size-gadget window and scales the video as that window is resized. RTG
(Half) opens that window at half the video's width and height and converts
H.264 straight to that size, which cuts colour conversion and the copy to
the card to about a quarter; other codecs play at full size.
The C2P chooser selects the standard graphics.library path, CD32 Akiko
hardware, or the Kalms converter for chipset playback (it is disabled for
CGX). Kalms is the default; incompatible geometry or bitmap layouts fall
back safely to graphics.library.

On a HAM6 or HAM8 screen whose height is an exact whole fraction of the
decoded height (the 640x360 into 640x180 shape a non-laced fit normally
produces), H.264 is converted straight from YUV to HAM pixel bytes rather
than through a full-resolution RGB24 frame - only the rows the downscale
keeps are converted, cutting that conversion by about 45%. Other HAM
geometries keep the established route.

  Play          starts the selected movie
  Pause         toggles playback
  Stop          exits the player
  Fast forward  toggles unpaced decode
  Volume -/+     adjusts Paula volume in 8/64 steps
  Playlist       opens the playlist window

The playlist window supports Add, Remove, Clear and Play, plus Load M3U
and Save M3U. Entries are shown by filename and can be selected
independently of the main file gadget; playback uses the same display,
audio and video options as the controller.

@{b}H.264 performance modes@{ub}

Turbo remains the default. Auto resolves to the same policy.

  Quality   full filtering and no deliberate frame skipping
  Balanced  in-loop deblocking disabled; motion compensation stays exact
  Fast      Balanced plus bilinear instead of six-tap interpolation;
            still keeps every frame
  Turbo     Fast policy plus B-frame skipping; keeps the P-frame chain
  Turbo+    skips both P- and B-frames, so only keyframes are decoded
            and shown, keeping that decode short enough to hold audio
            through it; last-resort keyframe/slideshow mode
  Smoosh    Turbo's policy, plus P/B pictures that are already late are
            not decoded at all: motion smears ("datamosh") until the next
            keyframe restores the picture, but the video keeps moving and
            audio comes first. Meant for streams with no B-frames, such as
            YouTube 360p, where Turbo has nothing to skip

Use Fast when keeping every frame matters more than maximum speed. Every
mode from Balanced down disables deblocking on keyframes as well as other
pictures, because leaving some pictures undegraded corrupted the decoder's
per-macroblock deblocking state (it made Fast slower than Quality). All of
these modes are substantially faster than they were in 1.2.0.

@{i}TurboGT@{ui} was retired in 1.3.1: it had been identical to Turbo's own
policy since that all-or-nothing deblocking fix, so it no longer selects
anything different. @{i}--h264-speed=turbogt@{ui} (and @{i}turbo-gt@{ui})
still work from the command line or a saved setting, aliased straight to
Turbo, but neither GUI offers TurboGT as a choice any more.

@{b}Fullscreen (RTG/CGX only)@{ub}

Press @{b}F@{ub} to switch the live player between its resizeable window
and a borderless public-screen-sized view, without restarting decoding;
@{i}--fullscreen@{ui} starts in that view directly. Press F again - or use
ytgui's Fullscreen button - to restore the previous window geometry. AGA
display modes remain hotkey-driven and ignore the RTG-only fullscreen
command.

@{b}Seeking@{ub}

Cursor left/right seek 10 seconds at a time for local QuickTime MOV/MP4
files, landing on the nearest keyframe via the sample index - a real seek,
not a fast-decode approximation. AVI, MKV, and network/live sources don't
have a keyframe index yet, so cursor-right stays the fast-forward toggle
for those instead. Cursor up/down adjust Paula's volume, the same 8/64
steps as ytgui's Vol -/Vol + buttons.

@{b}Playhead@{ub}

Once playback starts, the controller's Info: field mirrors a live
H:MM:SS/M:SS playhead in the current stream's own timeline, refreshed
about once a second, for local files and any stream played through the
IPTV/YouTube browsers - so a seek shows where it actually landed. On RTG
(CGX/P96), the same playhead also appears in the video window's own title
bar, visible even with the controller window elsewhere.

@{b}Every GUI@{ub} has a MintVID title-bar menu with @{b}Guide...@{ub}
(this manual), @{b}About MintVID...@{ub} with the project credits and
support link, and @{b}Quit@{ub} to close that frontend cleanly. The
controller's file gadget identifies the selected file and now also
remembers the last drawer a video was picked from, reopening there next
time instead of always starting fresh. On launch mrplay reports the
container type, video codec/FourCC, dimensions, frame rate and audio
format to its console.

For video timing choices see
  @{" Video frame policy " LINK FramePolicy}
and for complete hardware starting points see
  @{" Recommended settings " LINK Recommended}.
For audio output settings (rate, mono, no-audio, Fast RAM buffering) see
@{" Audio " LINK Audio}.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode


@node FramePolicy "Video frame policy"
@toc MAIN
                            Video frame policy
                            ------------------

The video selector is available in both the ReAction and GadTools
controllers:

  @{b}Video: All Frames@{ub}
      Keep every decoded picture, even when the decoder has fallen behind
      the presentation clock. This is the default GUI setting and the
      best starting point when playback is keeping up.

  @{b}Video: Skip Frames@{ub}
      Drop decoded pictures that are already late, allowing the scheduler
      to catch up while the audio clock and media timeline continue
      normally. This does not change the compressed stream or make the
      decoder cheaper; it is a presentation-time recovery policy.

  @{b}Video: Off@{ub}
      Play the soundtrack only. No video is decoded or shown, so a machine
      too slow for the picture (a YouTube talk, a music video) can still
      play it. A small window on Workbench shows the position: ESC or its
      close gadget stops, space pauses, cursor left/right seek and
      up/down change the volume. The controller's buttons work as usual.

Use @{b}Skip Frames@{ub} when a slower Amiga is visibly falling behind,
particularly with H.264, larger pictures, or a demanding display mode. It
can make motion less complete, but it is preferable to building an ever
larger delay when the machine cannot decode and display every picture in
time. Leave it on @{b}All Frames@{ub} for a source that is already smooth,
or when every decoded picture matters more than catching up.

The command-line equivalents are:

  --throughput       keep late decoded pictures (All Frames)
  --no-throughput    drop late decoded pictures (Skip Frames)
  --skip-trigger=MS  how far behind Skip Frames may fall first (Skip after)
  --no-video         audio only (Video: Off)

@{b}Skip after@{ub}, beside the Video chooser, sets how far behind a Skip
Frames session may fall (0.2 to 2 seconds, default 0.7) before it starts
skipping. It is greyed out for All Frames, Off and VQ Smoosh, which never
use it.

For most codecs this is a presentation policy: already-decoded late
pictures are discarded. H.264 gains a second, deliberately stronger
recovery step. When Skip Frames remains well behind, MintVID escalates
libavc to IVD_SKIP_PB, avoiding most P/B-picture decoding until the next
IDR. This reduces real decoder work rather than merely suppressing RGB
output. It was confirmed with a deliberately overloaded 720p stream under
WinUAE, where full-rate decoding was not possible.

Audio servicing, timestamps and seeking remain unchanged. A GUI-launched
session always passes an explicit choice so the controller selection
overrides mrplay's source-specific default. A direct network/HLS mrplay
invocation defaults to throughput mode; a direct local-file invocation
remains conservative unless you choose one of the switches explicitly.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode


@node Recommended "Recommended settings"
@toc MAIN
                           Recommended settings
                           --------------------

There is no single setting that makes every codec real time. Start with
the CPU-matched release drawer, keep the complete drawer together, and
change one setting at a time. The following are sensible starting points:

  @{u}68030 ECS/AGA@{uu}
    Display: AGA, lace off, 2x off
    C2P: Standard/automatic fallback
    Video: All Frames for light codecs;
           Skip Frames if playback falls behind
    Audio: Normal; use Low or Mono only when the CPU is under pressure
    Media: modest Cinepak, MPEG-1, MJPEG or other lightweight files

  @{u}68040 or 68060 with AGA@{uu}
    Display: AGA, HAM6 or HAM8; lace off, 2x off
    C2P: Kalms is the normal starting point
    H.264: Turbo first; Turbo+ is the audio-first fallback
    Video: All Frames when smooth; Skip Frames when decode is late
    Audio: Normal; try Low/Mono for a difficult H.264 stream

  @{u}68040 or 68060 with RTG@{uu}
    Display: CGX or P96
    C2P: ignored by RTG
    H.264: Turbo first; use a lower-resolution source before degrading
    Video: All Frames when the source keeps up; Skip Frames to recover
    Fast buffer: Auto for local files and direct/progressive HTTP media
    On a board with real hardware video overlay (Voodoo3/Permedia/BVision
      class - typically via Mediator or a PCI/Zorro bridge), P96 already
      tries to use it automatically, offloading scaling to the graphics
      card - confirmed working on real Voodoo3 hardware. It falls back to
      the older direct-write P96 path on its own if a board doesn't support
      it, so there is nothing separate to select.

  @{u}PiStorm/Emu68@{uu}
    Build: MintVID040
    Display: RTG (Half) for YouTube 360p (tested on a Pi3-based PiStorm
             600); CGX/P96 for smaller sources
    H.264: Turbo; Smoosh if high-motion scenes fall behind; Fast if
           full quality is affordable
    Video: All Frames is normally appropriate
    Fast buffer: Auto; increase only when the source and free RAM
                 justify it

  @{u}Live HLS/IPTV@{uu}
    Start with the GUI's All Frames, the default display mode, and the
    source's low-resolution HLS variant.
    Use Skip Frames only when the video is repeatedly falling behind.
    For a direct mrplay command, --live-resync can recover from a live
      stall; --net-queue=24 provides deeper read-ahead when free RAM
      allows it.
    HTTPS requires an SSL=1 build; SSLCERTS=1 is recommended for releases.

Keep lace and 2x off until the basic picture is working. They increase the
display workload and are not a universal quality improvement. If the
window is smooth but audio breaks up, try Audio: Low, Mono audio, or No
audio before changing several video controls together.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node GUIEditions "The GUI editions"
@toc MAIN
                             The GUI editions
                             ----------------

@{b}Two Workbench-friendly GUI sets@{ub} run over the same player,
parsers, playback settings, and status/control protocol:

  - @{b}MintVID@{ub}, iptvgui, ytgui use ReAction V44.
  - @{b}MintVID-GT@{ub}, iptvgui-GT, ytgui-GT use only GadTools/Intuition
    V37, for a standard AmigaOS 3.0 installation. Start MintVID-GT; it
    opens the matching -GT browsers automatically.

Keep one complete GUI set beside mrplay (or put mrplay on the command
path). Both editions drive the same underlying mrplay process and the same
options, so behaviour matches between them - only the front end differs.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node IPTVBrowser "IPTV browser"
@toc MAIN
                               IPTV browser
                               ------------

@{b}Live channel directory@{ub}

Both controllers include an IPTV... launcher for their matching directory
window. It reads valid cached channels.json and streams.json from
PROGDIR:Cache/IPTV/ immediately; the default public directory is iptv-org
(channels.json, streams.json, countries.json, categories.json).

MintVID does not host or redistribute television channels: iptv-org is a
collection of publicly available links, and individual links may be
offline, geo-blocked, or require request headers.

Cached JSON is used immediately, refreshed after 24 hours, and replaced
only after a complete download parses successfully - a failed refresh
leaves the prior cache intact. Manual HTTP/HTTPS media URLs, M3U8
playlists, and simple #EXTM3U lists use the normal MintVID URL/player
pipeline; playback still depends on the existing demuxers and codecs. HLS
prefers supported low-resolution variants (640 px wide by default) and
cannot make DRM, login-only, unsupported-codec, or dead streams playable.

Classic 68040/060 H.264 results depend heavily on the exact stream and
output mode. With the 1.2.0 optimisations, the lowest-resolution BBC One
HLS stream has approached real time on a tested real 68060 using AGA/HAM8,
while higher resolutions remain beyond normal classic hardware.
PiStorm/Emu68 remains the practical tier for broader H.264 IPTV playback.

Only the selected country's channels are held in RAM; unrelated global
streams are validated and discarded. Each channel retains up to four
preferred stream URLs, and the window's Next Stream button advances
through them without silently looping. Double-clicking a channel plays it
directly, without a separate Play press.

IPTV playback inherits a snapshot of the ReAction controller's display,
C2P, lace, and 2x selections when IPTV... is pressed; close and reopen the
window after changing controller settings to pick up new ones. A
Shell-launched iptvgui uses safe AGA/Standard, lace-off, 2x-off,
low-bandwidth HLS defaults.

Building with SSL=1 enables the iptv-org directory download over HTTPS;
without it, the browser stays usable for cached data and manual URLs, and
a refresh explicitly reports that it needs rebuilding with SSL=1.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node YouTube "YouTube"
@toc MAIN
                                 YouTube
                                 -------

@{b}Native YouTube playback@{ub}

mrplay can resolve a public YouTube Live watch/share URL natively. It
fetches the watch page with a browser user agent, extracts the signed HLS
manifest URL, validates it, and hands it to the normal HLS variant/segment
pipeline. Resolution happens on the Amiga itself, so IP-bound signed URLs
are not borrowed from a remote service.

For ordinary uploads, the resolver also tries YouTube's muxed 360p MP4
and, where still supplied, muxed 720p MP4 - both contain H.264 video and
AAC audio together, so MintVID's existing seekable HTTP/MP4 path plays
them without downloading or merging separate streams. Only a direct signed
HTTPS Google Video URL is accepted; ciphered URLs and unresolved player
challenges are rejected. Selecting 720p, 1080p, or Best tries 720p first
and falls back to 360p automatically; Low, 360p, and 480p stay on the 360p
muxed format. There is no standard muxed 480p or 1080p target yet.

On classic 68040/060 machines H.264 remains highly dependent on the stream
and output mode. The lowest-resolution BBC One HLS stream has approached
real time on a tested real 68060 with AGA/HAM8 after the 1.2.0 work, but
ordinary 360p uploads and higher resolutions remain beyond normal classic
hardware. PiStorm/Emu68 is the practical target for the broader YouTube
range.

This support is intentionally narrow: age/login/region-restricted videos,
DRM, uploads without a usable muxed format, and YouTube's private-schema
changes can all produce a clean unsupported error rather than a crash.

@{b}Searching@{ub}

The ReAction controller's YouTube... button opens the separate ytgui
search window. It searches YouTube's public results page without an API
key, shows the title and channel, and starts the selected result through
the same native resolver. The Quality button cycles through Low, 360p,
480p, 720p, 1080p, and unrestricted Best. The search-type selector
defaults to Live and also offers All, Videos, Shorts, and Hashtags. For
Hashtags, enter a single tag with or without the leading #; MintVID opens
YouTube's dedicated hashtag page rather than treating it as an ordinary
text search. Selecting a result and pressing Channel videos follows its
channel ID to that channel's public uploads list.

The transport row controls the separate player process directly: Play
first cleanly replaces the current video, Pause and Fast toggle their
modes, Vol -/+ adjusts Paula in steps, Fullscreen toggles the RTG window,
and Stop exits the player. Double-clicking a result plays it directly,
without a separate Play press.

Build with SSL=1 and keep ytgui beside MintVID and mrplay.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Audio "Audio"
@toc MAIN
                                  Audio
                                  -----

@{b}Audio output settings@{ub}

  Audio rate    Normal, or Low to halve Paula's output rate again -
                noticeably telephone-like, but a real CPU saving when
                H.264 decode is starving the audio FIFO
  No audio      skip the audio decoder and Paula entirely
  Mono audio    ask the codec for one channel instead of decoding both
                - MP3/MP2/AC-3 skip roughly half their per-channel work
                this way; Helix AAC only saves the downmix, since it has
                no mono decode mode. The one channel plays on both
                speakers.
  Fast buffer   Auto/Off/4/8/16/32/64 MB of Fast RAM read-ahead for
                local files and direct/progressive HTTP media. Auto
                picks 16/8/4 MB from the largest available Fast RAM
                block while leaving 24 MB free for decode/display/audio/
                queues; 32 and 64 MB are explicit choices. A fixed size
                still leaves an 8 MB floor and steps down safely if
                necessary. For YouTube and IPTV HLS the size bounds a
                queue of downloaded segments ahead of playback (up to
                eight for recorded videos, three for live streams).

Audio plays in stereo through a synchronized pair of Paula channels. A
stereo source keeps its left and right channels; a mono source, or Mono
audio, plays the same signal on both speakers. When the source rate is
above what Paula can play, or Audio is Low, AAC is decoded directly at
the lower rate with a smaller transform instead of decoding at full rate
and dropping samples.

MintAMP/Helix decode MP3, AAC-LC and fixed-point AC-3; MPEG-1/2 Layer II
(MP2) audio decodes through the same pl_mpeg path used for MPEG video. PCM
is also supported directly.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Live "Live streaming and networking"
@toc MAIN
                      Live streaming and networking
                      -----------------------------

Direct AVI/MOV/MP4 URL playback needs a finite, byte-addressable server:
Content-Length or Content-Range, and byte-range support when the container
seeks. MPEG-TS also accepts a forward-only chunked response. HLS playlists
use their own dedicated live/VOD path. Fragmented MP4 is not supported
yet.

For constrained hardware on live HLS:

  --net-queue=N    decoded-frame read-ahead target (default 1, ceiling
                    48). A deep value such as 24 lets video sit ahead
                    of the audio clock, keeps the audio FIFO fed, and
                    rides across a segment-boundary refetch, at the
                    cost of one RGB frame of RAM per used slot.
  --live-resync     recovers from a stall of more than ~4s behind the
                    wall clock by fast-consuming the backlog and
                    re-priming near the live edge, and reopens the
                    stream if it drops out entirely instead of ending
                    playback. Never fires in normal playback.
                    GUI-launched playback (MintVID, the IPTV browser)
                    enables this by default, since IPTV streams are
                    always live; a direct "mrplay <url>" leaves it off.

Plain HTTP is always available; HTTPS needs a build with SSL=1
(amisslmaster.library/AmiSSL v5) - add SSLCERTS=1 for certificate
verification, the recommended setting for a packaged release build.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node CLI "Command line (mrplay)"
@toc MAIN
                          Command line (mrplay)
                          ---------------------

Every GUI is a front end over mrplay, which also runs directly:

  mrplay "http://example.net/video.avi"
  mrplay "https://example.net/video.mp4"
  mrplay --user-agent "Mozilla/5.0" --referer "https://example.net/" \
    "https://example.net/live/master.m3u8"
  mrplay --hls-max-width=640 --hls-max-height=360 \
    "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=LIVE_STREAM_ID"
  mrplay --fast-buffer=8 "DH0:Videos/movie.avi"
  mrplay --net-queue=24 --live-resync \
    "https://example.net/live/master.m3u8"
  mrplay --p96 "DH0:Videos/movie.avi"
  mrplay --rtg-half --h264-speed=turbo "DH0:Videos/clip.mp4"
  mrplay --no-video "DH0:Videos/concert.mp4"

mrplay carries a $STACK:320000 AmigaOS stack cookie because libavc needs
substantially more stack than the classic Shell default - see
@{" Troubleshooting " LINK Troubleshooting} if a system ignores it.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Formats "Supported formats"
@toc MAIN
                            Supported formats
                            -----------------

@{b}Containers@{ub}: AVI, QuickTime MOV/MP4, Matroska/MKV, MPEG-TS/M2TS,
raw MPEG-1 program streams, raw MJPEG/MPEG-4 Visual streams, and direct
HTTP(S)/HLS URLs (including live IPTV playlists and YouTube).

@{b}Video codecs@{ub}: Cinepak, Microsoft Video 1, Microsoft RLE8,
uncompressed UYVY422, H.263 / H.263+, Motion-JPEG, MPEG-1, MPEG-2 (with
B-frames), MPEG-4 Part 2 (ISO ASP and Microsoft MP42/DIV2), Windows Media
Video 7 (WMV1), Windows Media Video 8 (WMV2), H.264/AVC Baseline/Main/
High Profile, and DV (IEC 61834/SMPTE 314M, PAL 4:2:0 and NTSC/DVCPRO
4:1:1).

WMV1's low-bitrate spatial intra/inter prediction mode is deliberately
rejected rather than approximated. WMV2 IntraX8 (J-frame) coding is also
not supported. Normal tested WMV1/WMV2 AVI I/P streams use the native
decoders.

@{b}Audio to Paula@{ub}: PCM, MP2, MP3, AAC-LC (raw/ADTS/LATM), and
fixed-point AC-3 (stereo downmix). MP3/AAC decoding reuses the
MintAMP/Helix code.

MintVID's core decoders are regression-tested against ffmpeg using the
project's test fixtures where applicable. The portable core is also
cross-built and exercised on a big-endian m68k target under QEMU to catch
endianness and alignment problems that a little-endian x86 host build can
miss.

MintVID uses a modular decoder architecture rather than being tied to one
fixed format, so the supported codec list can continue to grow. It is not
a video editor or encoder: use ffmpeg or similar on a modern machine to
prepare source material, then play the result on the Amiga.

For the detailed per-FourCC breakdown see
@{" Codec support list " LINK CodecList}.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node CodecList "Codec support list"
@toc MAIN
                            Codec support list
                            ------------------

Status marks what the decoder accepts and has been validated against
ffmpeg and/or real m68k hardware, not a speed guarantee - see
@{" Overview " LINK Overview} for what to expect from a given machine.

@{u}Video@{uu}

  Cinepak (CVID)                    accepted, ffmpeg-validated
  Microsoft Video 1 (MSVC/CRAM)     accepted, native 8/16-bit decoder
  Microsoft RLE8 (palettised AVI)   accepted, native decoder
  MJPEG                             accepted, ffmpeg-validated
  MPEG-1 video                      accepted, ffmpeg-validated
  MPEG-2 Main Profile (+ B-frames)  accepted, libmpeg2
  MPEG-4 Part 2 (ISO ASP)           accepted, ffmpeg-validated
  Microsoft MPEG-4 v2 (DIV2/MP42)   accepted, kept separate from ISO ASP
  Microsoft MPEG-4 v1 (DIV1/MP41)   not supported
  MPEG-4 v3 / DivX 3 (DIV3/MP43...) not supported; never routed to ASP
  H.263 v1 and H.263+ (partial)     accepted; unsupported H.263+ tools
                                    (SAC, 4MV, B pictures, PB, RPS, ISD,
                                    the deblocking filter, and others)
                                    are named and rejected, not
                                    approximated
  WMV1 / Windows Media Video 7      accepted, native decoder
  WMV2 / Windows Media Video 8      accepted; IntraX8 ("J-frame") mode
                                    explicitly rejected
  Raw UYVY422 (2vuy/UYVY)           accepted, uncompressed QuickTime/MOV
  H.264 High Profile (avc1)         accepted, libavc, CABAC + B-frames
  DV (IEC 61834/SMPTE 314M)         accepted, PAL 4:2:0 + NTSC/DVCPRO
                                    4:1:1, native libdv-derived decoder
  H.261, Indeo 3, Sorenson 1, VP3   not supported (Indeo/Sorenson/VP3
                                    planned)

@{u}Containers@{uu}

  AVI, QuickTime MOV/MP4, Matroska/MKV, MPEG-TS/M2TS, raw MPEG-1 program
  streams, raw MJPEG and raw MPEG-4 Visual - all packet-streamed from
  disk or HTTP(S), never loaded whole into RAM.

@{u}Audio@{uu}

  PCM, MP3 (all three MPEG Layer III versions incl. MPEG-2.5), MP2
  (MPEG-1/2 Layer II), AAC-LC (ADTS or LATM), and fixed-point AC-3
  stereo downmix - all reach Paula. AC-3 and .mpg MP2 are checked
  sample-by-sample against ffmpeg's own decode. --audio-mono is
  decoder-side for MP3/MP2/AC-3 and post-decode for AAC.

Codec tags (FourCCs) are matched case-insensitively, so a muxer's own
capitalisation never matters - AVI's fccHandler is the worst offender in
practice.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Troubleshooting "Troubleshooting"
@toc MAIN
                             Troubleshooting
                             ---------------

@{b}"Could not load iptvgui / ytgui / mrplay"@{ub}

Keep the whole release drawer together - MintVID, MintVID-GT, mrplay,
iptvgui, ytgui and the -GT set all expect to find each other
PROGDIR:-relative, in the same directory they shipped in.

@{b}Stack overflows / crashes under libavc (H.264)@{ub}

mrplay carries a $STACK:320000 AmigaOS stack cookie because libavc needs
substantially more stack than the classic Shell default. On systems that
don't honour stack cookies, run "Stack 320000" before starting the player.

@{b}HTTPS/YouTube/IPTV refresh says it needs SSL=1@{ub}

That build was compiled without AmiSSL support. Rebuild with SSL=1 (and
SSLCERTS=1 to verify server certificates), or use cached IPTV data and
plain HTTP URLs in the meantime.

@{b}A stream won't play@{ub}

MintVID cannot make DRM, login-only, geo-blocked, or unsupported-codec
streams playable - that is a property of the source, not a bug to report.
Direct AVI/MOV/MP4 URLs also need a finite, byte-addressable resource: the
server must supply Content-Length or Content-Range and honour byte ranges
when the container seeks.

If none of the above explains what you're seeing, see
@{" Reporting a problem " LINK ReportingProblems}.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node ReportingProblems "Reporting a problem"
@toc MAIN
                           Reporting a problem
                           -------------------

Please open an issue on the project's GitHub page and include:

  1. The exact command line or GUI steps used to reproduce it (media
     path/URL, display mode, C2P chooser setting, laced/2x, H.264 speed
     mode), and which release drawer (MintVID030/040/060) and CPU/board
     you ran it on - PiStorm/Emu68, Vampire/Apollo, or real 68k silicon.

  2. mrplay's own console output from that run. On launch it already
     reports the container type, video codec/FourCC, dimensions, frame
     rate and audio format - re-run with @{i}--time@{ui} added if the
     problem looks performance-related; that also prints which display/
     C2P implementation actually ran (for example @{i}kalms-040@{ui},
     @{i}kalms-2x2@{ui}, @{i}wpa@{ui}) and a per-stage timing breakdown.

  3. For a crash: the exact AmigaOS "Software Failure" text (task name
     and error number), and whether it happened on real hardware,
     PiStorm/Emu68, or an emulator such as WinUAE.

  4. For a YouTube/IPTV problem: the public video/channel URL or IPTV
     directory entry, since MintVID's YouTube/HLS resolution behaviour
     is often specific to one video, channel, or region.

MintVID's codecs are regression-tested against ffmpeg on the host and
cross-built for real big-endian m68k under QEMU (see CLAUDE.md in the
source tree) before anything ships, but the Amiga-specific display, audio
and GUI layers can only be verified on real hardware or PiStorm - so a
clear, reproducible report from actual hardware is genuinely the fastest
path to a fix.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

@node Licensing "Licensing"
@toc MAIN
                                Licensing
                                ---------

@{b}MintVID's own code@{ub} (the portable core, the Amiga display/audio
layers, and every GUI frontend) is MIT-licensed and available from its
project repository.

It links several dependencies that keep their own licences, and
distributing a @{i}built binary@{ui} means complying with all of them at
once, not just MIT:

  - libmpeg2 (VideoLAN) and the fixed-point Rockbox/a52dec AC-3 core are
    GPL-2.0-or-later, and are statically linked into mrplay. That makes
    the compiled binary a combined work under GPL-2.0-or-later -
    redistributing binaries obliges you to also offer corresponding
    source, per the GPL, even though MintVID's own contribution is MIT.
  - Ittiam libavc (H.264) is Apache-2.0.
  - MintAMP/Helix is a separately licensed submodule; retain its notices.
    In particular the Helix AAC decoder path it pulls in is licensed under
    RealNetworks' RPSL, not GPL.

This player was inspired by RiVA 0.54 (Fellner/Toeroek/Richter), the
fastest 68k MPEG-1 player, previously distributed on Aminet - but is a
fresh, unrelated codebase and does not include or depend on RiVA's source.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK "MAIN"}
@endnode

